// Practice quiz: Destructuring and spread
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/destructuring-and-spread

::o1 default and null::const \{ port \= 80 \} \= \{ port\: null \}; What is port? {
=null #Correct. Defaults apply only when the value is undefined.
~80 #Not quite. That would need port to be missing or undefined.
~undefined #Not quite. The property exists and holds null.
~A TypeError is thrown #Not quite. Destructuring a null property value is fine; only destructuring null or undefined itself throws.
}

::o1 position::const [, second] \= ["a", "b", "c"]; What is second? {
=b #Correct. The empty slot skips the first position.
~a #Not quite. The comma skips position zero.
~c #Not quite. Only one position is skipped.
~undefined #Not quite. Position one holds b.
}

::o2 merge order::const r \= \{ ...\{ a\: 1 \}, ...\{ a\: 2 \} \}; What is r.a? {
=2 #Correct. Later spreads overwrite earlier properties with the same key.
~1 #Not quite. The first value is overwritten.
~An array of both values #Not quite. Properties do not collect multiple values.
~undefined #Not quite. The key exists after the merge.
}

::o2 spread creates new::const a \= [1]; const b \= [...a]; b.push(2); What is a? {
=[1] #Correct. b is a new array, so its push does not touch a.
~[1, 2] #Not quite. That would happen if b were the same array.
~[2] #Not quite. Nothing removes the original item.
~A TypeError is thrown #Not quite. a is const, but nothing reassigns it.
}

::o3 shallow::const a \= \{ tags\: ["x"] \}; const b \= \{ ...a \}; b.tags.push("y"); What is a.tags? {
=["x", "y"] #Correct. The tags array is shared because spread is shallow.
~["x"] #Not quite. That needs a deep copy.
~undefined #Not quite. a still has its tags property.
~["y"] #Not quite. push adds to the existing items.
}
